, "kmlb_top19": 163}}, "notes": ["Lightning is NLDN cloud-to-ground plus GOES GLM total lightning (intracloud and cloud-to-cloud flashes as well), the 30 minutes before each sample. Hours whose GLM files were incomplete use NLDN alone (see Coverage); GLM locates flashes to ~8 km, coarser than NLDN.", "Isotherm heights come from the MRMS model freezing level and an assumed lapse rate; historical BUFKIT soundings are not available.", "Anvil exception part (a) uses the 0 C slice, not measured echo bases.", "Detached-anvil origin uses the previous hour, not the previous 5-minute frames.", "Satellite cloud tops (GOES band 13) can raise a cumulus class over radar echo - never add cloud, never lower a class. Their HEIGHT is derived from the band-13 temperature on the MRMS freezing level and a 6.5 C/km lapse rate for all years, because the 2 km height product only begins in March 2023; one method keeps the record consistent.", "Detached-anvil drift is steered by GOES band-14 anvil-level winds where available (band 8, then 32 kt in every direction, as fallbacks).", "GOES-16 is used before 7 Apr 2025 and GOES-19 from then on.", "Field mills (LLCC 4.1.2): violating if any one-minute |E| >= 1000 V/m from a mill within 5 nmi of the pad in the 15 minutes before; watch within 7 nmi. Field-mill exceptions in other rules are not modelled - they only relieve a violation, so this is the conservative side.", "With field mills included, only hours with mill data count, for every rule, so the period is the field-mill record's.", "One fixed classifier version across all years; MRMS itself was updated several times over the period.", "Radar is the KMLB + MRMS hybrid with single-source columns: a map column comes wholly from KMLB Level II where KMLB samples it from 3 km or lower up to 15 km, and wholly from MRMS everywhere else - never a mix of the two within a column. Each hour uses the KMLB volume whose scan midpoint is within 6 minutes of the sample; hours without one are scored on MRMS alone (see Coverage).", "With AVSET on, KMLB usually stops at 6.4 deg, about 6.8 km over the pads, so pad columns are then MRMS; they are KMLB mainly when AVSET is off for launch countdowns or deep convection keeps it scanning. 'Full depth over the pads' in Coverage is the share of hours whose LC-39A column came from KMLB.", "KMLB quality control: below 3 km, gates under 35 dBZ with RHOHV below 0.8, or under 20 dBZ with RHOHV missing, are removed as clutter; everywhere, isolated patches under 20 gates and 20 dBZ are removed as speckle. MRMS applies its own, different QC.", "The VII 'core' test uses KMLB VII floored at 0.5 kg/m2 where KMLB samples the -10 to -40 C layer - an approximation of the MRMS product.", "Same rules, isotherms, lightning and field mills as the MRMS climatology, so the two records differ only in the radar."], "kmlb": true}